---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout title="About this archive">
  <h1>About this archive</h1>
  <p>
    <strong>www.rhubarbinfo.com</strong> — “The Rhubarb Compendium” —
    was a long-running site devoted to rhubarb in all its forms: recipes,
    growing tips, varieties, history, and the kind of folk knowledge that
    only accretes when one person tends one corner of the web for a long
    time. After 2024-10-23 the domain stopped resolving as expected and
    is now a casino-redirect squat.
  </p>

  <p>
    This site is a non-commercial preservation effort. Every page here is
    a clean re-render of an
    <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/2024*/rhubarbinfo.com">Internet Archive</a>
    snapshot, with the original URL and archive timestamp linked at the top of
    each entry. Where Wayback held a snapshot for an image, that image was
    pulled at the snapshot closest to the page; nothing here is fabricated.
  </p>

  <h2>How it was rebuilt</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The Wayback CDX API was used to enumerate every captured URL on
        <code>rhubarbinfo.com</code> with HTTP 200 status before 2024-10-24.</li>
    <li>For each unique URL, the latest snapshot was fetched via
        <code>web.archive.org/web/&lt;ts&gt;id_/&lt;url&gt;</code> (the
        <code>id_</code> flag returns the unmodified original bytes).</li>
    <li>HTML was parsed and converted to Markdown, preserving the body
        content while stripping site-chrome and Blogger / Drupal infrastructure.</li>
    <li>External images (Blogger CDN) were re-fetched via Wayback
        closest-snapshot and re-hosted alongside the text.</li>
    <li>The whole pipeline lives in <code>tools/</code> in the
        <a href="https://github.com/maphew/rhubarb">source repository</a>
        — the rescue is reproducible.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Three eras</h2>
  <p>
    The captured snapshots span three eras of the site:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Static (2000-2006)</strong> — pre-CMS HTML hosted on
        FreeServers / Tripod. Most surviving pages from this era are
        bookstore links and some early recipes.</li>
    <li><strong>Drupal (2007-2020)</strong> — “The Rhubarb Compendium” on
        Drupal, with deep recipe taxonomy and category pages.</li>
    <li><strong>Blogger (2019-2024)</strong> — the final incarnation,
        with cleaner long-form articles and the most recent edits.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Credit</h2>
  <p>
    All credit for the original content belongs to the original author(s)
    of <code>www.rhubarbinfo.com</code>. If you are the original author and
    would like attribution updated or content removed, please open an issue
    on <a href="https://github.com/maphew/rhubarb">github.com/maphew/rhubarb</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
